  "title": "Experiments on transformation thermodynamics: Molding the flow of heat",
  "authors": "Robert Schittny et al., 2013",
  "central_proposition": "The time-dependent heat conduction equation's form-invariance under curvilinear coordinate transformations allows for the experimental realization of a micro-structured thermal cloak that molds heat flow around an object, providing transient protection while maintaining undisturbed downstream heat flow.",
  "concept_definitions": [
    {
      "name": "Transformation Thermodynamics",
      "definition": "A design tool that maps fictitious transformed space onto (meta-)materials with spatially inhomogeneous and anisotropic heat-conductivity tensors in laboratory space, based on the form-invariance of the time-dependent heat conduction equation under curvilinear coordinate transformations."
    },
    {
      "name": "Thermal Cloak",
      "definition": "A metamaterial structure designed using transformation thermodynamics to mold the flow of heat around an object, protecting it from heating while ensuring the heat flow downstream remains undisturbed, as if the object and cloak were absent."
    },
    {
      "name": "Form-invariance",
      "definition": "The property of an underlying physical equation (e.g., time-dependent heat conduction) to retain its mathematical form under curvilinear coordinate transformations, enabling the mapping of curved space to material properties."
    }
  ],
  "argument_structure": "The paper first introduces the theoretical basis of transformation thermodynamics, drawing an analogy to transformation optics. It then details the design principles for a thermal cloak based on this theory, including the derivation of required anisotropic and inhomogeneous heat conductivities. The fabrication process of a micro-structured copper-PDMS metamaterial is described, followed by an experimental setup for transient heat flow measurements using an infrared camera. Finally, experimental results demonstrating the cloaking effect are presented and compared with numerical simulations, validating the theoretical predictions.",
  "mechanism_or_causal_logic": "The core mechanism relies on the form-invariance of the time-dependent heat conduction equation under curvilinear coordinate transformations. By applying a coordinate transformation that creates an 'empty' region in a fictitious space, the corresponding material properties (specifically, the heat-conductivity tensor) in the physical laboratory space become spatially inhomogeneous and anisotropic. This engineered material distribution effectively guides heat around the cloaked region, making the heat flow outside the cloak appear undisturbed, as if the cloaked object were not present. The specific anisotropy and inhomogeneity are achieved by a metamaterial design of alternating high and low conductivity rings (copper and PDMS) with varying filling fractions.",
